# Test fixtures are byte-exact oracles: gzipped .wrl (binary), plain/gzip twins
# compared byte-for-byte, deliberate CRLF twins, and PNG textures. Git for
# Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites plain-text .wrl
# fixtures to CRLF on checkout — that broke the "gzipped .wrl decompresses to
# text identical to its plain twin" comparison on Windows (Phase 7C5). Mark the
# whole fixture tree as -text so every fixture byte is identical on all
# platforms regardless of a checkout's autocrlf setting.
test/fixtures/** -text
spikes/xite-mall-fit/fixtures/** -text

# App-icon assets must be byte-identical on every platform. The four owner-approved
# source SVGs are the owner's exact bytes (LF) and the deterministic generator
# relies on identical input to reproduce identical output; the generated PNG/ICO
# are binary. Marking them -text stops Git-for-Windows autocrlf from EOL-rewriting
# the SVGs on checkout (which changes their on-disk bytes) and from ever touching
# the binaries. See docs/ICONS.md.
assets/wrl-forge-*.svg -text
assets/generated/icons/** -text

# The VRML engine core must check out as LF on every platform. Git for Windows'
# core.autocrlf=true rewrites plain-text source to CRLF on checkout; that broke
# the generated node-schema.js LF-only invariant and made two node-identity.js
# source-inspection tests misread `//` comments (their per-line strip regex
# anchors on `$`/`.`, neither of which matches a trailing `\r`) on Windows CI.
# `text` (not `-text`) because this is text that should still get Git's normal
# text handling -- only the EOL is pinned, unlike the byte-exact fixtures above.
src/vrml/** text eol=lf
